I approve the generic exact-repeat guard for plan 008, not an edit-only one, with the conditions below. The v2 numbers trace to results/plan007_arm1_summary.json. I checked d' (4/33), unscreened_25 (3/25), dec060_identical_runs (17 tasks with a run ≥5, 11 of the 12 empties) and dec062 (0 blocks). 4/33 stays provisional until review 133120 comes back clean (dec 069 item 2 still applies).

1. FEASIBILITY FIRST (blocking, before any build). Your message says "the tool returns a 'you already did this' message". But eval/plans/007-repeat-guard.md l.11 says the bundle is declarative, and that we can't add a Python callback or tool wrapper. The same question applies to the dec 069 (i) submit_patch empty-diff guard. Every guard has to live inside the submitted bundle: agent.yaml, prompts, skills, or a sub-agent via agent_tool. Nothing may be patched into our local copy of the harness, because the scorer won't have it, and a local-only guard would make arm 2 measure something we can't ship. Check what the scorer harness actually lets a bundle configure (grep adk_submission/schema.py and agent.yaml loading for tool config, callbacks and tool-response hooks). Write the answer in one paragraph at the top of eval/plans/008.md, citing file:line.
   - If tool-level hooks really are configurable in the bundle: build the tool-level guards as you proposed.
   - If they aren't (the expected case): implement both guards as prompt rules in prompts/system.md. (a) Use Option A text from the design note for repeats. (b) Before submit_patch, the agent must check that `git diff --stat` shows at least one non-test source file changed, and if not, edit a source file first. It may skip that check only when the remaining call budget can't fit an edit, and it must never submit an empty diff on purpose. Label the plan PROMPT-LEVEL so the mechanism metrics are read as compliance, not enforcement.
2. Scope: the guard covers all tools (same tool + byte-identical args, consecutive). edit_file failures are included, and so is the dec 069 (ii) edit-failure fallback (after 2 failed edit_file calls on the same file, use a shorter anchor or write_file). Everything stays generic, with no repo, file or task wording (dec 057). Fold in the NB2 pipefail fix at system.md:33.
3. Add a second MECHANISM metric to plan 008: tasks whose max identical consecutive run is ≥5, using the dec060 definition from the arm-1 summary and the same script. Base 17/33; expect ≤6/33. The first mechanism metric is unchanged: empty-patch 12/33, expect ≤4/33. Add one SAFETY metric: the count of submitted-without-any-test-run, paired against arm 1 (design note Risk 1). It must not rise by more than 3. PRIMARY, the ship rule and the k=1 power caveat are as in dec 069 item 3. The ship rule now needs BOTH mechanism metrics to be met, unless the plan pre-states that a large drop in the repeat metric alone is enough. Pick one now and write it down before any data.
4. Everything in dec 069 item 4 is unchanged: CPU only today, a v010 smoke of ≤2 tasks only if pack and review are done by about 22:00Z, the full arm 2 after the reset and after the dec 055 31b-v002 submit at 2026-11-07T00:00Z, which goes FIRST.
5. Arm 3 note: arm 1 had 0 thinking blocks in 33 tasks, so thinking was effectively off. In the arm-3 addendum (due 2026-11-07T18:00Z), confirm that the thinking-on config actually produces blocks in the 2-task smoke before the full arm runs. If it doesn't, arm 3 is invalid by construction.

Reasoning: the generic guard fits the data better, since only 3 of the 17 looping tasks loop on edit_file, and loops come with 11 of the 12 empties. The real risk is building a guard the scorer can't run. One grep settles that before we spend a build cycle or quota. Unscreened 3/25 against screened 1/8 shows no sign of overfitting to the screen ids.
